//@version=6
// Indicator declaration:
//   - "Alpha Relative Strength & Performance" is the full name shown in the indicator list
//   - "Alpha RSP" is the short name displayed on the chart pane
//   - overlay=false places the indicator in a separate sub-panel below the price chart
//   - max_lines_count=500 allows up to 500 line objects for potential future extensions
indicator("Alpha Relative Strength & Performance [Pineify]", "Alpha RSP [Pineify]", overlay=false, max_lines_count=500)

// =========================================================================
// INPUTS
// =========================================================================
// Benchmark symbol: the reference index that the current asset is compared against.
// Common choices: SPY (S&P 500 ETF), QQQ (Nasdaq 100), BTCUSD (Bitcoin), etc.
benchmark = input.symbol("SPY", "Benchmark Symbol", tooltip="Index to compare against (e.g., SPY, BTCUSD)")

// Calculation window: the lookback period used for WMA smoothing of the RS ratio and momentum.
// Shorter values (e.g., 7) react faster but are noisier; longer values (e.g., 50) are smoother but lag more.
length = input.int(14, "Calculation Window", minval=2, maxval=200)

// Colors - Four-Quadrant System
// Each color corresponds to one of the four relative strength quadrants:
//   Green  = Strong (Leading)   — asset outperforms benchmark with rising momentum
//   Blue   = Improving          — asset underperforms but momentum is turning positive
//   Yellow = Weakening          — asset outperforms but momentum is fading
//   Red    = Weak (Lagging)     — asset underperforms with falling momentum
colorStrong = input.color(#089981, "Strong (Leading)", inline="col1")
colorImprove = input.color(#2962ff, "Improving", inline="col1")
colorWeakening = input.color(color.yellow, "Weakening", inline="col2")
colorWeak = input.color(#F23645, "Weak (Lagging)", inline="col2")

// =========================================================================
// DATA FETCHING & CALCULATIONS
// =========================================================================
// Fetch the benchmark's closing price from the previous bar.
// Using close[1] with lookahead_on avoids repainting by ensuring we only use
// confirmed (already closed) benchmark data — never the current in-progress bar.
benchClose = request.security(benchmark, timeframe.period, close[1], lookahead=barmerge.lookahead_on)

// Guard against missing benchmark data (e.g., symbol not found, no data on this bar).
// All subsequent calculations check this flag and fall back to neutral values if false.
validData = not na(benchClose)

// --- 1. Relative Strength Ratio & Momentum Calculation ---

// RS (Relative Strength) = current asset price / benchmark price.
// A rising RS means the asset is gaining value faster (or losing slower) than the benchmark.
// Falls back to 1.0 (neutral) when benchmark data is unavailable.
rs = validData ? close / benchClose : 1.0

// Smooth the raw RS with a Weighted Moving Average (WMA).
// WMA gives more weight to recent bars, providing a responsive yet stable trend line of RS.
wma_rs = ta.wma(rs, length)

// RS Ratio: measures where the current RS sits relative to its own smoothed trend.
// Formula: WMA(RS / WMA_RS) * 100
//   - Values > 100 → asset is outperforming its own recent average (leading the benchmark)
//   - Values < 100 → asset is underperforming its own recent average (lagging the benchmark)
// The double WMA smoothing (first on RS, then on the ratio) reduces noise significantly.
rsRatioRaw = ta.wma(rs / wma_rs, length) * 100
rs_ratio = validData ? rsRatioRaw : 100.0

// RS Momentum: measures the rate of change of the RS Ratio itself.
// Formula: RS_Ratio / WMA(RS_Ratio) * 100
//   - Values > 100 → the RS Ratio is accelerating (momentum is positive / improving)
//   - Values < 100 → the RS Ratio is decelerating (momentum is negative / deteriorating)
// This is the "second derivative" of relative strength — it tells you whether
// outperformance/underperformance is getting better or worse.
rsMomRaw = rs_ratio / ta.wma(rs_ratio, length) * 100
rs_mom = validData ? rsMomRaw : 100.0

// --- 2. Relative Performance (Net Returns Delta) ---

// Calculate single-bar percentage returns for both the asset and the benchmark.
// These are simple arithmetic returns: (current - previous) / previous.
assetReturn = validData ? (close - close[1]) / close[1] : 0.0
benchReturn = validData ? (benchClose - benchClose[1]) / benchClose[1] : 0.0

// Net Performance = Asset Return - Benchmark Return, expressed in percentage points.
// Positive → asset outperformed the benchmark on this bar.
// Negative → asset underperformed the benchmark on this bar.
// This provides a bar-by-bar "alpha" reading independent of the smoothed RS calculations.
netPerformance = (assetReturn - benchReturn) * 100

// =========================================================================
// STATE DETERMINATION & COLORING
// =========================================================================
// Classify the asset into one of four quadrants based on RS Ratio and RS Momentum.
// The 100 level is the equilibrium line for both metrics:
//   RS Ratio > 100  → "Leading"  (asset outperforms benchmark trend)
//   RS Ratio < 100  → "Lagging"  (asset underperforms benchmark trend)
//   RS Mom   > 100  → "Positive Momentum" (relative strength is improving)
//   RS Mom   < 100  → "Negative Momentum" (relative strength is deteriorating)
isLeading = rs_ratio > 100
isPositiveMom = rs_mom > 100

// Four-quadrant state assignment (inspired by RRG — Relative Rotation Graphs):
//   Strong (Green)     = Leading + Positive Momentum  → best quadrant, full outperformance
//   Weakening (Yellow) = Leading + Negative Momentum  → still ahead but losing steam
//   Improving (Blue)   = Lagging + Positive Momentum  → behind but catching up
//   Weak (Red)         = Lagging + Negative Momentum  → worst quadrant, full underperformance
// Assets typically rotate clockwise through these quadrants over time.
stateColor = isLeading and isPositiveMom ? colorStrong :
             isLeading and not isPositiveMom ? colorWeakening :
             not isLeading and isPositiveMom ? colorImprove :
             colorWeak

// Human-readable state label used in the info table display.
stateText = isLeading and isPositiveMom ? "Strong (Leading)" : isLeading and not isPositiveMom ? "Weakening" : not isLeading and isPositiveMom ? "Improving" : "Weak (Lagging)"

// =========================================================================
// BEAUTIFUL PLOTTING
// =========================================================================
// Dashed horizontal line at 100 — the equilibrium baseline.
// RS Ratio above this line = outperforming; below = underperforming.
hline(100, "Equilibrium Baseline", color=color.new(color.gray, 50), linestyle=hline.style_dashed)

// Plot the RS Ratio as a thick line colored by the current quadrant state.
// This is the primary visual element of the indicator.
rsPlot = plot(rs_ratio, "RS-Ratio", color=stateColor, linewidth=3)

// Invisible baseline plot at 100 — used solely as an anchor for the fill below.
basePlot = plot(100, "Base", display=display.none)

// Semi-transparent fill between the RS Ratio line and the 100 baseline.
// Visually highlights the magnitude of outperformance (above 100) or underperformance (below 100).
fill(rsPlot, basePlot, color=color.new(stateColor, 85), title="Performance Area")

// (Commented out) Histogram showing bar-by-bar net performance delta.
// Uncomment to add a column-style overlay showing single-bar alpha.
// perfPlot = plot(netPerformance + 100, "Net Performance Delta %", style=plot.style_columns, color=netPerformance > 0 ? color.new(colorStrong, 60) : color.new(colorWeak, 60))

// =========================================================================
// CROSS SIGNALS
// =========================================================================
// Bullish Entry Signal:
//   Triggers when RS Ratio crosses ABOVE 100 (asset begins outperforming)
//   AND the previous bar had positive momentum (RS Mom > 100).
//   This combination ensures we enter only when the asset is transitioning
//   into the "Strong" quadrant with momentum already confirmed.
strongCross = ta.crossover(rs_ratio, 100) and isPositiveMom[1]

// Bearish Exit Signal:
//   Triggers when RS Ratio crosses BELOW 100 (asset begins underperforming)
//   AND the previous bar had negative momentum (RS Mom < 100).
//   This combination ensures we exit only when the asset is transitioning
//   into the "Weak" quadrant with deteriorating momentum already confirmed.
weakCross = ta.crossunder(rs_ratio, 100) and not isPositiveMom[1]

// Plot small circles at the RS Ratio level where crossover signals occur.
// Green circle = Bullish Entry, Red circle = Bearish Exit.
plotshape(strongCross ? rs_ratio : na, title="Bullish Entry", style=shape.circle, location=location.absolute, color=color.new(colorStrong, 0), size=size.tiny)
plotshape(weakCross ? rs_ratio : na, title="Bearish Exit", style=shape.circle, location=location.absolute, color=color.new(colorWeak, 0), size=size.tiny)

// =========================================================================
// DISPLAY INFO TABLE
// =========================================================================
// Create a 2-column × 4-row table anchored to the bottom-right corner of the pane.
// This table provides a real-time dashboard of the asset's relative strength metrics.
var table infoTable = table.new(position.bottom_right, 2, 4, bgcolor=color.new(color.gray, 90), border_width=1)

// Populate the table only on the last (most recent) bar to avoid redundant processing.
if barstate.islast
    // Row 1: Current quadrant state (Strong / Weakening / Improving / Weak)
    table.cell(infoTable, 0, 0, "State:", text_color=color.white, bgcolor=color.gray)
    table.cell(infoTable, 1, 0, stateText, text_color=stateColor, bgcolor=color.new(stateColor, 80))
    
    // Row 2: RS Ratio value — above 100 = leading, below 100 = lagging
    table.cell(infoTable, 0, 1, "RS Ratio:", text_color=color.white, bgcolor=color.gray)
    table.cell(infoTable, 1, 1, str.tostring(rs_ratio, "#.00"), text_color=stateColor, bgcolor=color.new(stateColor, 80))
    
    // Row 3: RS Momentum value — above 100 = improving, below 100 = deteriorating
    table.cell(infoTable, 0, 2, "RS Momentum:", text_color=color.white, bgcolor=color.gray)
    momColor = isPositiveMom ? colorStrong : colorWeak
    table.cell(infoTable, 1, 2, str.tostring(rs_mom, "#.00"), text_color=momColor, bgcolor=color.new(momColor, 80))
    
    // Row 4: Net Performance — single-bar alpha (asset return minus benchmark return)
    table.cell(infoTable, 0, 3, "Net Perf:", text_color=color.white, bgcolor=color.gray)
    perfColor = netPerformance >= 0 ? colorStrong : colorWeak
    table.cell(infoTable, 1, 3, str.tostring(netPerformance, "+#0.00;-#0.00") + "%", text_color=perfColor, bgcolor=color.new(perfColor, 80))

// =========================================================================
// ALERTS
// =========================================================================
// Alert conditions allow users to set up notifications in TradingView
// without needing to watch the chart continuously.

// Fires when a Bullish Entry crossover signal occurs (RS Ratio crosses above 100 with positive momentum).
alertcondition(strongCross, title="Alpha Strong - Bullish Entry", message="Alpha RSP: Bullish Entry - Asset starting to lead benchmark")

// Fires when a Bearish Exit crossover signal occurs (RS Ratio crosses below 100 with negative momentum).
alertcondition(weakCross, title="Alpha Weak - Bearish Exit", message="Alpha RSP: Bearish Exit - Asset falling behind benchmark")

// Fires on every bar where the asset is in the Strong quadrant (leading + positive momentum).
alertcondition(isLeading and isPositiveMom, title="Alpha Strong State", message="Alpha RSP: Asset is Strong (Leading with positive momentum)")

// Fires on every bar where the asset is in the Weak quadrant (lagging + negative momentum).
alertcondition(not isLeading and not isPositiveMom, title="Alpha Weak State", message="Alpha RSP: Asset is Weak (Lagging with negative momentum)")
